  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\n- Rain showers, low chance of thunder today and tonight\n\nSatellite water vapor channels and Airmass RGB product show a deep \nupper PV intrusion curling around the eastern flank of a vertically \nstacked low over east-central IL. This intrusion extends into far\nsouthwest Lower MI as well. There is some question regarding how \nmuch conditional instability we will realize this afternoon and \nevening. The general consensus is for parts of our forecast area, \nparticularly far SW Lower and south central Lower to make a run \ntowards 1000 J/kg MUCAPE. Insolation should be minimized by \nextensive mid/upper cloudiness associated with broad upper \ndeformation (and concomitant divergence) over most of southern \nLower MI.\n\nAs noted previously, we are in an environment characterized by \nseasonably large precipitable water values (greater than 90th \npercentile). When combined with light flow in the cloud-bearing \nlayer and a warm cloud depth well in excess of 10000ft AGL, we \nare looking at a setup with very high precipitation efficiency.\nThus, any showers or thunderstorms will be able to produce large\nrainfall rates with a risk of at least some mostly nuisance\nflooding.\n\nEarlier CAM guidance advertised a narrow, generally west to east \nband of precipitation developing this afternoon/evening across the \ncentral CWA. The forcing for this has been a bit uncertain but could \npossibly be related to a deep wind col over this area between the \nclosed upper low immediately southwest and an open trough immediately\nnortheast. In any case, newer CAM model solutions are collectively\nless consistent about developing this feature. What seems most \nplausible is that potentially heavy rain could brush the southeast\nforecast area, including Jackson County, later tonight into Sunday\nmorning in conjunction with low level fgen oriented northeast of \nthe progressing lower tropospheric low and this precipitation \ncould linger into early Sunday afternoon. This scenario agrees \nwith the WPC excessive rainfall forecast showing a greater flood \nrisk just south of the forecast area.\n\n\n- Dry Monday and Tuesday, then chances for rain\n\nForecast certainty is high (greater than 90 percent) for dry conditions\nMonday and Tuesday. By Tuesday night, a broad upper trough is \nexpected to be positioned over the northern Plains with a surface \nfront poised to enter the forecast area from the northwest. There \nis some question regarding the timing of this front reaching \nsouthern Lower MI; several of the deterministic solution indicate \nan upper PV max swinging through Wednesday evening, which in turn \nwould help with the southeastward progression of the front. The \ncoupled forcing for upward vertical motion supplied by the front \nand PV max will justify greater PoPs.\n\nAs noted before, we will be in a persistent broad upper troughing \npattern, meaning continued chances of showers and thunderstorms with \npoor predictability regarding timing and coverage. It is worth \nnoting that the latest available medium range machine learning and \nCFSv2 ensemble guidance point to increasing severe convection \nchances in the Friday/Saturday timeframe and an active pattern for \nstrong/severe convection that could persist into next week.",
